{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":40,"LineContent":"This report documents the full development of the \\code{compass\\_sim} project: a Python simulator for two-dimensional arrays of magnetic compass needles interacting through complete dipolar coupling, with inertial Newtonian dynamics in SI units. The project began as a pedagogical demonstration of the analogy between compass arrays and magnetic materials, including hysteresis and domain formation, and evolved over six development sessions into a research instrument with publication potential. The report records the project decisions, problems encountered and solutions adopted, the code-version history (V20--V56), the research and publication strategy built around a gap identified in the literature, and the results of the pilot campaign on ``damping~$\\times$~hysteresis/avalanches''. The pilot already shows the central signal sought: a systematic increase of the avalanche exponent $\\tau$ with the quality factor $Q$."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":53,"LineContent":"To build a visual simulation of an array of compass needles that rotate only under the magnetic field generated by each needle on its neighbors, seeking the collective directional equilibrium. The simulation provides a direct and intuitive mechanical analogy with real magnetic materials, capable of displaying hysteretic behavior and magnetic-domain formation from a top view and exportable as video."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":56,"LineContent":"To use the simulator, which implements real Newtonian \\emph{inertial} dynamics, a rare feature in this literature, to address a nonequilibrium statistical-physics question with publication potential:"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":62,"LineContent":"The literature has established that underdamped and overdamped systems belong to different universality classes in short-range or mean-field models, such as inertial Kuramoto models, damped Ising models, and sheared amorphous solids~\\cite{dahmen2011}. The identified gap is that no one has tested whether this picture survives real dipolar coupling, which is long-ranged ($\\sim 1/r^3$) and anisotropic, in two-dimensional arrays with continuous angular dynamics and physical inertia. As far as the literature review reached, the simulator developed here is the only instrument built specifically for this question."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":68,"LineContent":"Each needle $i$ is represented as a point magnetic dipole of moment $m$ fixed at a lattice position \\vec r_i, free to rotate in the plane around a central pin with no contact friction, but with viscous damping from air. The dynamics is Newton's second law for rotation:"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":74,"LineContent":"where $I$ is the needle's moment of inertia, $b$ is the viscous damping coefficient, and the torques come from $\\vec\\tau = \\vec m \\times \\vec B$. The dipolar field acting on needle $i$ sums the contributions from \\emph{all} other needles, using complete coupling with computational cost $\\mathcal{O}(K^2)$ for $K$ needles:"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":81,"LineContent":"The integration uses a Velocity--Verlet scheme with time step $\\Delta t$ proportional to the natural period $T_0 = 2\\pi/\\omega_0$, where"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":94,"LineContent":"A structuring decision was to use SI units and derive the parameters from the real needle geometry rather than arbitrary constants:"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":96,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Moment of inertia}: computed for a \\SI{0.4}{mm}-thick steel sheet shaped as a diamond needle, for example $I \\approx \\SI{3.8e-11}{kg.m^2}$ and mass $\\approx$ \\SI{0.017}{g} for a \\SI{0.5}{cm} needle;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":97,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Magnetic moment}: assuming steel saturated along the long axis, $m = M_s V$, with $M_s = \\SI{1.59e6}{A/m}$, corresponding to ordinary carbon steel with $B_{\\mathrm{sat}} \\approx \\SI{2.0}{T}$;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":103,"LineContent":"The simulator supports three lattice geometries (square, triangular, honeycomb); \\textbf{six} external-field modes: static; pulse with explicit timing (delay $\\Delta t$ $\\to$ field for $t_{\\mathrm{pulse}}$ $\\to$ zero field until equilibrium, with the legacy saturation/plateau criterion if $t_{\\mathrm{pulse}}$ is not given); five-segment hysteresis ($0 \\to +B \\to 0 \\to -B \\to 0 \\to +B$) with linear or log-symmetric spacing; sinusoidal field; and step modes \\code{step\\_pos} ($B{=}0$ for $\\Delta t$, then field kept on) and \\code{step\\_neg} (field from $t{=}0$, removed at $\\Delta t$). It also supports periodic boundary conditions with lattice images; real-time monitoring of the alignment order parameter $S \\in [0,1]$; a real-equilibrium criterion (small angular velocity \\emph{and} small mean torque) for pulse/step modes; optional GPU backend through CuPy; MP4 video export, summary images, and CSV time series $(t, B, M, S)$; magnetic-domain visualization through halo coloring; a precomputed dipolar tensor (V40); and performance instrumentation."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":113,"LineContent":"The first session established the core: traditional compass needles shaped as diamonds (blue north half, white south half), viewed from above; fully dipolar interaction among all needles; and the search for directional equilibrium. Successive requests added lattice parameters (spacing, $N \\times M$ count, square/triangular choice), honeycomb geometry (hexagon vertices), a uniform external field with controllable magnitude and direction, comments documenting all code sections, saving in the current directory, and the video pipeline (PNG frames at regular intervals assembled into MP4 through ffmpeg)."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":121,"LineContent":"\\textbf{(a) Interface and control}: visual indication of external-field direction and intensity in the image; automatic incremental numbering of videos (\\code{name0000.mp4}, \\code{0001}, \\dots); controlled interruption through \\mbox{Ctrl+I} or a physical criterion ($S = 1.00$); and explanation/exposure of the damping parameter and the quality factor $Q$."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":123,"LineContent":"\\textbf{(b) Time semantics}: \\code{t\\_sim} was redefined to mean the simulated \\emph{real physical time}, namely the sum of the integrated $\\Delta t$ values, equivalent to what a stopwatch would measure while observing the real system, rather than the wall time of the execution. This distinction led to successive corrections until the behavior became consistent."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":125,"LineContent":"\\textbf{(c) Honeycomb correction}: the initial implementation produced distorted networks that were not truly hexagonal. After several attempts, the adopted solution was to construct the honeycomb as a triangular lattice with systematic removal of the central site of each hexagon, and to parameterize all geometries by the radius $R$ of the circle circumscribing the needle. This guarantees a uniform $r_{nn} = 2R$ across geometries, which later proved essential for clean scientific comparison among them, with the same nominal $Q$ in all three lattices."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":129,"LineContent":"This phase transformed the demonstrator into a measurement instrument:"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":135,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{CSV export}: $(t, B, M, S)$ series with magnetization projected along the field direction, using the same numbering scheme as the videos. This became the backbone of the subsequent scientific analysis;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":139,"LineContent":"Periodic boundary conditions (PBC) were introduced at the end of this phase as an on/off parameter, then completed in the next phase with image replicas in the dipolar-field calculation (default: one copy in each direction)."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":143,"LineContent":"An organizational milestone was reached: by request, all previous versions were retroactively consolidated (V1--V19), and a \\textbf{formal version record} was established from \\textbf{V20} onward, with an increment for each change. This practice has been maintained since then and also adopted for this report."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":145,"LineContent":"This phase included refinement of the pulse mode; repositioning of on-screen indicators (field at the top right, with a dedicated upper margin to avoid overlap with needles); and \\textbf{GPU support} via CuPy, motivated by the availability of an NVIDIA RTX~3090 on the computing server. The investigation of why GPU utilization remained low led, in the next phase, to complete vectorization of the torque loop, eliminating interpreted nested Python loops with $\\mathcal{O}(K^2)$ cost, and to the honest conclusion that for small and medium arrays the dominant cost was not computation but rendering. This redirected the optimization effort."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":153,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Rendering optimization (V35)}: replacement of thousands of individual matplotlib calls by vectorized collections (\\emph{PatchCollections} + \\emph{quiver}), with a measured speedup of \\textbf{17--30$\\times$}: a $25\\times25$ array that exceeded 30~s of rendering dropped to 3.9~s, and $40\\times40$ (previously impractical) dropped to 14~s, with bitwise validation of polygon geometry and physics CSV;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":154,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Automatic magnetic moment (V37)} from the saturated volume ($M_s = \\SI{1.59e6}{A/m}$, researched for ordinary carbon steel), and correction of a regression where \\code{--progress\\_bar 0} also suppressed periodic status messages;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":155,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Magnetic-domain visualization (V38)}: halo coloring by domain. Neighboring needles whose directions differ by less than an angular tolerance (\\code{--domain\\_tol}, default $15^\\circ$) are grouped through \\emph{union--find}; small domains ($\u003c\\max(3;\\,2\\%)$ of the needles) are painted neutral gray so the main domains stand out (Figure~\\ref{fig:dominios}). This feature began as visualization and became a measurement instrument: the same routine feeds the domain-size statistics of the scientific campaign."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":172,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{\\code{--make\\_images} (V39)}: it was found that, even without \\code{--video}, the program unconditionally generated 4--5 summary PNG images per run. This cost is negligible in large arrays, but dominant in small arrays used for sweeps. The new parameter suppresses all image generation while preserving data CSVs, with a measured speedup of $\\sim 3\\times$ in small runs (2.03~s $\\to$ 0.65~s for an $8\\times8$ array);"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":174,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Performance optimizations implemented (04/07)}: the precomputed dipolar tensor (\\textbf{V40}) and campaign parallelization with incremental \\emph{manifest} and resume support (\\code{damping\\_sweep} V02). Details and measurements are in Section~\\ref{sec:melhorias}. The measured V40 speedup is \\textbf{35--45$\\times$} per run, reducing the full campaign from $\\sim$7--8~h to $\\sim$15~min on a single core;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":175,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{New field protocols and refinements (V41--V45)}: hysteresis with log-symmetric spacing; pulse with explicit timing ($\\Delta t$ + $t_{\\mathrm{pulse}}$ + torque equilibrium); step modes \\code{step\\_pos}/\\code{step\\_neg}; \\code{--t\\_sim\\_full} flag (run to the end without early stopping); halos also in the comparison figure (V43); a two-line live progress panel (V44); and hysteresis video with a dual progress panel (V45)."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":176,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Layout, terminal updates, and translations (V46--V56)}: docstring alignment (V46); autoscale parameter \\code{--hyst\\_autoscale} (V47); bugfixes and autoscale options (V48); HUD-needle overlap resolution and signed field bar updates (V49); progress bar English translation alongside a three-line console print layout (V50); HUD legend layout alignment alongside a constant-length field arrow position update (V51); full translation of all remaining terminal console prints and confirmations to English (V52); circular direction dial and sign indicator arrow next to the horizontal field bar updates (V53); terminal banner code version and developer metadata addition (V54); HUD direction dial arrow scaling with the removal of the sign indicator next to the bar (V55); and HUD direction dial arrow scaling minimum length calibration (V56)."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":186,"LineContent":"\\noindent The review covered three fronts. \\textbf{(i)}~Classical compass-needle arrays: the founding work (square $22\\times22$ lattice, Phys.\\ Rev.\\ B \\textbf{58}, 9238, 1998~\\cite{prb1998}) uses equilibrium Monte Carlo with a random field mimicking thermal noise, not Newtonian dynamics. \\textbf{(ii)}~Classical dipoles in honeycomb/kagome/triangular lattices (2012--2025): Luttinger--Tisza methods, energy minimization, spin waves, and Monte Carlo with \\emph{parallel tempering}, all focused on ground states and thermal \\emph{equilibrium} transitions. \\textbf{(iii)}~\\emph{Artificial spin ice}~\\cite{nisoli2013}: real temporal dynamics is studied, but through discrete macrospins (Ising-like) or experimental data (MOKE/MFM), almost never with continuous angular dynamics and explicit mechanical inertia."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":194,"LineContent":"The decision criterion was defined \\emph{a priori}:"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":196,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Result A (incremental, solid)}: $\\tau$ approximately constant with $Q$ (only scales/cutoffs change), consistent with known phenomenology and publishable in the standard target journals;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":197,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Result B (surprising)}: $\\tau$ varies systematically with $Q$, or there is a qualitative difference between geometries, signaling a breakdown of universality due to anisotropic long-range dipolar coupling. This would justify attempting PRL or a similar venue."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":203,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{CAPES--ACS agreement}: it was confirmed that the CAPES transformative \\emph{Read \\\u0026 Publish} agreement covers \\textbf{100\\% of the APC in any ACS journal} (CC~BY license) for corresponding authors from consortium institutions, with eligibility checked during submission via ORCID, removing cost as a decision factor;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":204,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Primary target}: \\emph{ACS Materials Au} (IF~6.5, Q2), the highest-impact fully \\emph{open access} ACS option. The paper must be framed as a model for \\emph{artificial spin ice} and patterned nanomagnets, which was incorporated into the introduction design;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":206,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{High-impact alternatives}: \\emph{Physical Review Letters} is \\emph{outside} the CAPES agreement (APS is not a participating publisher); \\emph{Nature Communications}, being \\emph{gold OA}, is excluded from \\emph{Read \\\u0026 Publish} agreements (APC $\\sim$ USD~6,190 separately). Both are justified only for a type-B result;"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":207,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Other CAPES publishers} (Springer Nature $\\sim$1,750 hybrid titles, Elsevier $\\sim$160, Wiley, IEEE, RSP, ACM): a partial review did not find, up to letter~H of the Springer list, a title with better fit than the ACS targets; natural Elsevier candidates (\\emph{Physica A}, \\emph{JMMM}) await verification in the eligible-title list."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":217,"LineContent":"\\textbf{\\code{damping\\_sweep.py}} orchestrates the campaign by calling the simulator programmatically without modifying the source code. It builds the log-spaced damping grid in $Q$ from the real physics (inertia and moment derived from geometry), runs Step~1 (hysteresis) and Step~2 (free relaxation with domain statistics) for each geometry~$\\times$~$Q$~$\\times$~seed combination, and saves per-run CSVs, JSON metadata (including $Q$, $\\omega_0$, $B_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, domain distribution), and a consolidated \\code{manifest.csv}."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":219,"LineContent":"\\textbf{\\code{damping\\_sweep\\_analysis.py}} reads \\code{manifest.csv} and produces: loop area (closed-line integral), coercivity and remanence (interpolation at zero crossings); avalanche detection in $dM/dB$ within each monotonic ramp segment (robust threshold of $4\\times$MAD); power-law fitting by MLE with $x_{\\min}$ selected by KS distance~\\cite{clauset2009}, \\emph{aggregating avalanches from all seeds} for each $(geometry, Q)$ point before fitting, a methodological choice required for reliable statistics; and final plots, including the central $\\tau(Q)$ test by geometry."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":221,"LineContent":"Validations performed: area against an exact rectangular loop; $B_c$/$M_r$ against a synthetic loop with known values; avalanche detection against injected steps (size recovery with $\\sim$1\\% error); MLE against a synthetic sample with exact $\\tau = 2.5$ (recovered $2.516 \\pm 0.021$), through both code paths (the \\code{powerlaw} package, with a real bug in the installed version corrected, and the in-house fallback implementation, with identical results)."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":225,"LineContent":"A reduced pilot (array $14\\times14 = 196$ needles, 5 $Q$ values, 3 seeds, 3 geometries, shortened cycle) validated the full pipeline and already displays the central signal sought (Figure~\\ref{fig:tau} and Table~\\ref{tab:piloto}): the exponent $\\tau$ \\textbf{increases systematically with $Q$} in all three geometries, from $\\tau \\approx 1.2$--$1.3$ in the \\emph{crossover} ($Q \\approx 0.87$) to $\\tau \\approx 1.7$--$1.8$ in the underdamped regime ($Q = 15$). For the two most overdamped $Q$ values, the shortened pilot cycle does not generate enough avalanches for fitting, which is the correct pipeline behavior: it reports unavailability instead of a spurious number."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":248,"LineContent":"\\caption{Central test of the study (pilot): avalanche power-law exponent $\\tau$ as a function of the quality factor $Q$, by geometry. The systematic increase of $\\tau$ with $Q$, if confirmed in the full campaign with adequate statistics, is the candidate signature that the universality class depends on the damping regime under dipolar coupling.}"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":255,"LineContent":"\\caption{Pilot: hysteresis-loop area as a function of $Q$, by geometry. The area increases monotonically when entering the overdamped regime (more dissipation per cycle), with the three geometries qualitatively similar in this metric, unlike domain size, for which the triangular lattice shows non-monotonic behavior to be investigated.}"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":259,"LineContent":"Two secondary findings from the pilot are worth recording: (i) loop area and coercive field increase monotonically when entering the overdamped regime (Figure~\\ref{fig:area}); (ii) the mean domain size in the \\emph{triangular} lattice shows non-monotonicity with $Q$ that is not seen in the other geometries, exactly the kind of frustration~$\\times$~inertia interaction identified in the study design as a type-B candidate result. Both require the full campaign for confirmation."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":263,"LineContent":"Defined parameters: $30\\times30$ lattice (900 needles), 8 log-spaced $Q$ values in $[0.05;\\,15]$, 5 seeds, 3 geometries $\\Rightarrow$ 120 hysteresis runs + 120 relaxation runs. Cost with V39 code: $\\sim$2--2.5~min per run ($\\approx 7$--8~h serial campaign). \\textbf{With V40 optimization} (precomputed tensor, Section~\\ref{sec:melhorias}): $\\sim$3.3--3.4~s per run $\\Rightarrow$ \\textbf{$\\sim$15~min serial campaign on a single core}, and only a few minutes with \\code{--n\\_workers} on the \\code{jps@mumax} server, with no GPU required."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":270,"LineContent":"This section details, in operational format, the four studies identified as publication candidates, from the central study to the satellites. For each study it specifies the question, simulation steps, analysis, expected result, and editorial destination. All use only already implemented capabilities (V39 + campaign scripts), except where indicated."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":272,"LineContent":"\\subsection{Study 1 (central): damping as a control parameter for hysteresis and avalanches}"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":282,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Decision and writing}: A/B criterion from Section~\\ref{sec:pesquisa}; framing as a ``model for \\emph{artificial spin ice} / patterned nanomagnets'' in the introduction."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":285,"LineContent":"\\textbf{Expected result.} The pilot suggests increasing $\\tau$ with $Q$ in the three geometries. If confirmed with full statistics and surviving robustness diagnostics, this is a type-B candidate result. \\textbf{Destination:} ACS Materials Au (result A) or an attempt at PRL (result B), with ACS Physical Chemistry Au as backup."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":297,"LineContent":"\\textbf{Destination:} may form part of the central article (domain section) or, if the frustration~$\\times$~inertia interaction is rich, a separate article in ACS Physical Chemistry Au. Computational cost is low, since no field ramps are required."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":299,"LineContent":"\\subsection{Study 3 (satellite): memory of the \\emph{director} after a pulse}"}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V22_EN.tex","LineNumber":301,"LineContent":"\\textbf{Question.} After a saturating field pulse, does the \\emph{director} (emergent preferred direction, concept from the 1998 work~\\cite{prb1998}) formed during relaxation retain memory of the pulse direction, or is it erased and redetermined by the initial noise? Equilibrium Monte Carlo cannot answer this, since it requires a real time trajectory."}
