{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V17_EN.tex","LineNumber":186,"LineContent":"The review covered three fronts. \\textbf{(i)}~Classical compass-needle arrays: the founding work (square $22\\times22$ lattice, Phys.\\ Rev.\\ B \\textbf{58}, 9238, 1998~\\cite{prb1998}) uses equilibrium Monte Carlo with a random field mimicking thermal noise, not Newtonian dynamics. \\textbf{(ii)}~Classical dipoles in honeycomb/kagome/triangular lattices (2012--2025): Luttinger--Tisza methods, energy minimization, spin waves, and Monte Carlo with \\emph{parallel tempering}, all focused on ground states and thermal \\emph{equilibrium} transitions. \\textbf{(iii)}~\\emph{Artificial spin ice}~\\cite{nisoli2013}: real temporal dynamics is studied, but through discrete macrospins (Ising-like) or experimental data (MOKE/MFM), almost never with continuous angular dynamics and explicit mechanical inertia."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V17_EN.tex","LineNumber":301,"LineContent":"\\textbf{Question.} After a saturating field pulse, does the \\emph{director} (emergent preferred direction, concept from the 1998 work~\\cite{prb1998}) formed during relaxation retain memory of the pulse direction, or is it erased and redetermined by the initial noise? Equilibrium Monte Carlo cannot answer this, since it requires a real time trajectory."}
{"File":"/Users/jps/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/simula agulhas/relatorio_projeto_V17_EN.tex","LineNumber":342,"LineContent":"\\item \\textbf{Adaptive time step in the overdamped regime (proposed; investigate cautiously).} With $Q \\ll 1$, the dynamics is slow and $\\Delta t$ tied to $T_0$ is conservative. A larger step, limited by the viscous scale, could reduce the cost of the slowest runs. This requires a dedicated convergence study before adoption. With the V40 gain, it is no longer a priority."}
